message:
{{#is_alert}}Production average cost per model call over the last 4h is over $0.075 — 4x the ~$0.018/call production baseline. Something changed the price, not the volume.{{/is_alert}}
{{#is_warning}}Production average cost per model call over the last 4h is over $0.05 — ~2.7x baseline, and above the busiest legitimate day measured ($0.034); worth a look at what ran.{{/is_warning}}

This is the price signal the daily spend guardrail cannot see: a runaway loop, a broken prompt-cache prefix (RC1-350 — pr-review-agent uncached is ~$0.06/call vs ~$0.015 cached), a model swap, or prompt growth all raise cost per call; a busy shipping day does not.

Triage on the [fleet dashboard](https://app.datadoghq.com/dashboard/bwm-uny-qqs):
- **Prompt caching row**: pr-review-agent cache hit % should sit near 80% during a review; near 0% means the shared prefix broke.
- **Cost by model**: a sonnet subject suddenly on opus shows here first.
- **Cost by ml_app**: the kpi-agent's opus define/instrument/narrate calls are legitimately ~$0.08–0.12 each — a handful in one window can reach the warn line; that is the one known benign trigger.

**Population (RC1-411).** Numerator and denominator both exclude `service:evals` and `service:dry-run`. Corpus traffic is high-volume and cheap per call, so including it *diluted* this average and desensitized the monitor: on 2026-09-06 the blended figure read $0.0149/call while production actually ran $0.0341/call. The contamination was hiding price spikes, not creating them.

Query: cost / clamp_min(calls, 10) / 1e9 — the denominator floors at 10 calls so one expensive call cannot trip it on its own. Thresholds set from the trailing-7d mean on 2026-09-03 (RC1-377), re-derived on 2026-09-08 (RC1-411) against the production-only series: window mean $0.0184/call, recent days ~$0.013, legitimate peaks $0.034. The old $0.035/$0.05 pair sat *below* two known-good days once corpus traffic was removed.

